.TH OD 1 "September 19, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
od \- dump file bytes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B od
.RB [ \-bcdosvx ]
.RB [ \-A " base" ]
.RB [ \-t " type" ]
.RB [ \-j " skip" ]
.RB [ \-N " limit" ]
.RI [ file ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Od
dumps a file with the origin of the line on the left.
This byte-machine version prints sixteen bytes per line and uses a
four-digit hexadecimal offset, rather than PDP-11 octal words.
With no file it reads standard input.
A final line gives the trailing offset alone.
.TP
.B \-x
Hexadecimal bytes; also the default.
.TP
.B \-b
Three-digit octal bytes.
.TP
.B \-c
Printable characters, with a dot for nonprinting bytes.
The high bit is stripped before classifying characters.
.TP
.B \-d
Unsigned decimal 16-bit words, same as -t u2.
.TP
.B \-o
Octal 16-bit words, same as -t o2.
.TP
.B \-s
Signed decimal 16-bit words, same as -t d2.
.TP
.B \-A base
Offset column in base d (5 digits), o (6) or x (4, the default);
n prints no offset.
.TP
.B \-t type
Output type, repeatable.
A type string is one or more of
.B a
(named characters, low seven bits),
.B c
(characters, with C escapes, else three octal digits),
and
.BR d ,
.BR o ,
.B u
or
.B x
(signed decimal, octal, unsigned decimal, hexadecimal)
followed by a size: 1, 2 or 4, or C, S, I, L (1, 2, 4, 4).
No size means 4.
There is no 8-byte unit: x8 and every other size is a usage error.
Units are little-endian and the last one of a short row is padded
with zero bytes.
Each type prints its own line under the same offset; later lines carry
a blank offset, and the columns are widened to line up.
.TP
.B \-v
Print every row.
Without it, a row equal to the one before collapses to a single
.B *
line for the whole run.
.TP
.B \-j skip
Read and discard skip bytes first.
.TP
.B \-N limit
Dump at most limit bytes.
.PP
Of the old letters, -x, -b and -c together print in hex, octal,
character order whatever the argument order;
-t, -d, -o and -s print in argument order.
Skip and limit are unsigned decimal operands.
.SH FILES
/usr/man/OD  this page
.SH "SEE ALSO"
cat(1), licenses(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0 on completion, 1 if the input cannot be opened, 2 for usage errors.
.SH BUGS
Offsets and numeric operands are 16-bit; do not use values above 65535.
Hexadecimal digits are upper case, as in the offset column.
-x is hexadecimal BYTES here, not the XCU's 16-bit words (use -t x2).
-c keeps the high bit stripped, so a text byte prints as itself where a
host od prints octal; -t c does the same for a byte whose low seven
bits are printable or a C escape.
No 8-byte or floating types.
